target:    tests/oracle/classify.py
gate:      composer test:classifier-plants
kind:      restore-defect
expect:    emits the PASS label 'a' from f.agrees() without requiring f.oracle_class in ANSWERED
rationale: Drops the ANSWERED term from new-backed-registered, which is how S4 shipped: OLD with rows,
           NEW hollow, ORACLE hollow, one register entry - and the verdict was (a)/PASS, NEW PROVEN
           CORRECT. The same hole S4-oracle-backs-new-by-being-empty-01 forbids on the sibling rule
           old-errored-registered; the repair was applied to one of the two rules emitting (a), and
           that entry's own rationale claimed this one always carried the term. It did not. Found by
           SWEEPING the class cross product through the real classify() in Run 55 - 114 of 114,048
           cases, reproduced independently at 4 of 700 - and by NO plant: all 50 were green with the
           defect present and stayed green when it was fixed. NOTE WHAT KILLS IT, because it is not an
           assertion: _assert_table_invariants() now refuses this at IMPORT, reading the guard's own
           bytecode via __code__.co_names, so the gate dies before a single plant runs and the expect:
           above is a ClassificationError rather than a check label. That is the point - the defect is
           unwritable now, not merely detected.
---
diff --git a/tests/oracle/classify.py b/tests/oracle/classify.py
--- a/tests/oracle/classify.py
+++ b/tests/oracle/classify.py
@@ -435,7 +435,7 @@ RULES = (
         "this surface with this observable. An expected diff — a number that changed on purpose. "
         "`oracle_class in ANSWERED` is not redundant beside `agrees()`: an oracle that returned "
         "nothing must not be able to prove NEW correct",
-        lambda f: (f.oracle_class in ANSWERED and f.agrees(f.oracle_new)
+        lambda f: (f.agrees(f.oracle_new)
                    and f.register_hit is not None),
     ),
     Rule(
